
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Project context: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m + [38;5;153mapp.js[39m are a single static page with a login form that stubs its API call; [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m
  and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are a separate CommonJS demo module. There's no build tooling, no test runner, no framework, no
  backend, and — relevant here — no settings in the app yet.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "persist across sessions" can mean two very different systems. Either preferences live in the browser and
  follow the device, or they live behind an API and follow the user account. That choice decides whether this is a
  ~100-line client module or a feature that needs a backend, and it sets the stored format and the read/write interface
  that everything else is written against — expensive to swap later.

  - [1mBrowser-local (localStorage)[0m — buys: works today, no server, no auth dependency, synchronous reads so the UI can
    apply prefs on first paint. Costs: per-device and per-browser, lost when storage is cleared, and anything stored is
    readable by any script on the page. Rules out cross-device sync without a later rewrite of the read path (async).
  - [1mServer-backed per account[0m — buys: real cross-device persistence tied to the user, survives cache clears, can hold
    non-cosmetic settings. Costs: there is no backend in this repo and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub returning a hardcoded success,
    so this means building an API, a store, and real auth first. Much larger scope than the request implies.
  - [1mLocal now, async-shaped interface[0m — buys: ships on localStorage but defines [38;5;153mgetPreference[39m/[38;5;153msetPreference[39m as async
    from day one, so a server backend later swaps behind the same calls. Costs: slightly awkward async for a synchronous
    store, and the seam is speculative until a backend actually exists.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-local with a plain synchronous interface.[0m There's no backend or real auth here to attach
  account-scoped preferences to, and the async seam is YAGNI — if a server arrives, it arrives with an auth system, and
  adapting a small, well-bounded module at that point is cheap compared to carrying the abstraction now.
